-- js-vocabulary.rofl — THE MODEL'S OPINIONS ABOUT KINDS, CHECKED AGAINST THE
-- LIST OF KINDS IT CLAIMS TO DESCRIBE.
--
-- `vocabulary_gap[audit]` in rules/js-model.rofl watches the CORPUS: a kind the
-- scanner emits that the vocabulary does not name. This file watches the
-- RULES, and the hole it closes was named by the ledger on 2026-09-08 and left
-- as a decision:
--
--     "Diffing the kinds a RULE READS BY NAME against the declared vocabulary
--      finds two: `program`, which is structural, and `class_expression` —
--      which `obj_like[flow]` and `node_value_kind` both read, so the model HAS
--      an opinion about it, and the matrix has NO CELL for it at any layer. A
--      kind the vocabulary does not declare can never be reported unmodelled,
--      so an opinion held in the rules is invisible to the ledger that exists
--      to count opinions."
--
-- The two audits fail in OPPOSITE directions and neither implies the other. A
-- kind can be in the corpus and in no rule (`vocabulary_gap` sees it, this file
-- does not); a kind can be in a rule and in no corpus (this file sees it,
-- `vocabulary_gap` does not) — which is exactly what `class_expression` was,
-- since the corpus that would contain one is written by whoever models it.
--
-- THIS IS THE FIRST RULE IN THE CORPUS THAT READS THE KERNEL'S OWN
-- REIFICATION. Every clause is stored as facts after parsing — that is how the
-- evaluator reads them — and `premise_lit(RuleId, K, Lit)` carries the whole
-- body element as a term: `$lit(Rel, Persp, $cons(A1, $cons(A2, ...)), Tense)`,
-- with an argument that is a variable appearing as `$var("Name")`. So the
-- question "which kinds does a rule name" is a QUERY rather than a script, and
-- the check lives in the language it checks.

-- WHAT A RULE'S BODY SAYS, WALKED ONE ARGUMENT AT A TIME. The reified argument
-- list is a cons chain and Datalog cannot index into one, so the walk is a
-- recursion that carries the position. `$not(...)` gets its own arm and is not
-- an afterthought: `not ast_node[code](X, with_statement, _, _)` is an OPINION
-- ABOUT `with_statement` in exactly the sense this audit is about, and the
-- first draft of this file saw only positive premises.
body_lit(L)         :- premise_lit(_, _, L), L = $lit(_, _, _, _).
body_lit(L)         :- premise_lit(_, _, $not(L)).
lit_rel(L, Rel)     :- body_lit(L), L = $lit(Rel, _, _, _).
arg_at(L, 1, Args)  :- body_lit(L), L = $lit(_, _, Args, _).
arg_at(L, J, T)     :- arg_at(L, I, $cons(_, T)), J is I + 1.
lit_arg(L, I, A)    :- arg_at(L, I, $cons(A, _)).

-- A CONSTANT, WHICH IS EVERYTHING THAT IS NOT A VARIABLE. There is no type test
-- among the kernel's builtins — `=`, `!=`, the four comparisons and `is` — so
-- the variable case is derived positively and subtracted, which is the same
-- move `slot_atom` would make in any language without one.
slot_term(Rel, I, A)      :- lit_rel(L, Rel), lit_arg(L, I, A).
slot_var(Rel, I, $var(V)) :- lit_rel(L, Rel), lit_arg(L, I, $var(V)).
slot_atom(Rel, I, A)      :- slot_term(Rel, I, A), not slot_var(Rel, I, A).

-- WHICH ARGUMENT POSITIONS CARRY A KIND — DISCOVERED, NOT DECLARED, and that
-- is the load-bearing choice in this file. A hand-written table of kind slots
-- is a list that rots silently: the day somebody adds a relation whose second
-- argument is a kind, a table nobody updated reports zero and means nothing.
-- Instead a position IS a kind position when a DECLARED kind appears in it, so
-- the audit configures itself from the rules it is auditing.
--
-- MEASURED 2026-09-08 on the five js rule packs: exactly two positions qualify,
-- `ast_node/2` with 39 declared kinds and `transfer_site/2` with 3. A hand
-- table would have had `ast_node` and missed `transfer_site`.
--
-- THE LIMIT IS STATABLE AND WORTH STATING: a position in which EVERY constant
-- is an undeclared kind is invisible to this rule, because nothing marks it as
-- a kind position. That is the price of not hand-writing the list, and it is
-- the cheaper mistake — a self-configuring check that misses a wholly new
-- relation beats a hand list that misses a wholly new relation AND rots.
kind_slot(Rel, I)     :- slot_atom(Rel, I, A), node_kind(js, A).
kind_named_by_rule(K) :- slot_atom(Rel, I, K), kind_slot(Rel, I).

-- AND THE VERDICT. The two exclusions are the ones `vocabulary_gap` already
-- uses, for the same reason and with the same guards against a misspelling:
-- `not_a_construct` for the scanner's own roots, `frame_deferred` for a kind
-- outside the frame with an owner. `program` is the only name this audit
-- reports on an honest tree and it is excluded by the first of them.
rule_opinion_unlisted[audit](Lang, K) :- kind_named_by_rule(K), lang_of_corpus(Lang),
                                         not node_kind(Lang, K),
                                         not not_a_construct(K),
                                         not frame_deferred(K, _).
